  "report_semantics": {
    "control_variate": "A coefficient estimated on policy-training pairs is frozen before valuation; the estimate subtracts that coefficient times the same-path European deviation from its analytic Black-Scholes expectation.",
    "crr_reference": "The high-step CRR adjacent average is an internal model cross-check. It is neither exact American truth nor market truth, and no count derived from it is an acceptance gate.",
    "deterministic_cases": "Cases whose valuation estimator has zero variance produce a single-point interval, so containment of any finite-step tree value is arithmetically impossible regardless of agreement. They are summarised separately from stochastic interval comparisons.",
    "estimand": "Each LSM price is the discounted value of a learned Bermudan stopping policy evaluated on valuation paths independent of the paths the policy was fitted on, over the declared discrete exercise grid.",
    "lower_estimate": "For a fixed learned policy the expectation is no greater than the same-grid optimal stopping value, so LSM sitting below the finer-step CRR reference is the expected ordering rather than a defect.",
    "standard_error": "Antithetic pair averages are the independent observations; individual paths are not counted as independent.",
    "valuation_interval": "The reported interval and standard error cover valuation sampling error only. They quantify noise around an already-frozen policy.",
    "variance_reduction": "Reported only where mathematically defined. Deterministic immediate-exercise cases have an undefined 0/0 ratio and are excluded from every variance-reduction summary, so they can neither set nor lower a reported minimum."
  },
